If your constraints are correct, NUnit will not throw an error, the tests will pass because the constraints define what should be the behavior (the method should increase the Count, should add a number to the end of the list and should maintain the sorted order of the elements), the observed values give a way to understand what is happening in the actual execution.

For more detailed information, I suggest you have a look at the documentation on the NUnit website.

Remember though, these tests are not meant to be fool-proof, rather they should be documentation in a way that lets you know what your code should do and how it's actually doing it under the hood. The actual behavior can still change for unforeseen reasons. Also, these tests are not intended to cover every corner case, they're just meant to give you an understanding of what is happening. You should then write more comprehensive unit tests that test edge cases and other tricky portions of your code.

I hope this helps, and best of luck with your project.
