A: Just to elaborate on the previous answer, the Code Contracts are a collection of lightweight design and assertion checks built directly into the compiled .NET Common Language Runtime (CLR). The purpose of Code Contracts is to provide a level of safety to the program and to assist analysis and debugging.
Code Contracts are not really designed for unit testing - usually the purpose of unit testing is to test methods by themselves, isolating them from external dependencies, like databases, UI, network, etc.
If you want to test the Add(x) method, it would be better to isolate it from all other parts and to test it independently. The idea of unit testing is to test each method (smallest unit) without depending on other methods. That is, your test cases should be focused and isolated not only on the Add(x) method but also on other methods or sections of the code base that use this method.
Now, if you have concerns about the Add(x) method and you want to do some post-conditions checking (i.e. what to do after the method has been called), Code Contracts might be a proper solution.
Finally, as for your question "How I can improve my test case coverage for the above mentioned class" - that really depends on what you define as 'high coverage'. Are you looking at lines of code, statements, methods, branches etc., using some specific tool that checks this automatically? Or are you looking for a way of just manually checking? Or are you aiming for test automation? All of those would be different goals so I would advise providing more specific requirements if this could help. 
Regards.

A: Unit tests (like what your code contract test is doing) are typically responsible for testing individual units of code in isolation from the rest of the system. This is to ensure that all of your units are working as intended. 
In your case, you're wanting to test the addition of an item to a list,--------------------            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX, "LastIndexOf of x must be geq than IndexOf of x.");
        }
